<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divriği Great Mosque and Hospital</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1228">1228</a>–<a href="/years/1229">1229</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divriği</a> Great Mosque and Hospital is a joined mosque and hospital in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, built in 1228–1229 by the Mengujekid ruler Ahmad Shah and his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turan-malik">Turan Malik</a>. It is famous for the richly carved stone portals, unlike any other Seljuk-era building. It was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1985.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divrigi-great-mosque-and-hospital.jpg" alt="Divriği Great Mosque and Hospital" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mxcil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Divrigi_Darussifa.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divriği</a> was founded in the 9th century under Byzantine rule. After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071 Turkish tribes occupied it, and in the 12th century the Mengujekid dynasty controlled it and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>. The dynasty later divided into two branches, one ruling from Divriği and the other from Erzincan, and it was related by marriage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. The Mengujekid rulers were patrons of arts, science and literature, which may explain why exceptional artists were present, and itinerant artisans moving from patron to patron probably gave rise to the eclectic architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex stands in the upper town, below the citadel. The mosque adjoins the hospital, with which it shares its southern qibla wall, and a mausoleum is attached to the hospital. The north portal of the mosque gives the date 626 AH (1228–1229) and names its patron as Ahmadshāh ibn Sulaymān, a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divrigi">Divriği</a> branch. The inscription on the hospital portal calls it a dār al-shifā' (house of healing) and ascribes its foundation to Tūrān Malik. The chief architect, Khurramshāh ibn Mughīth al-Khilātī, came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahlat">Ahlat</a>.</p><p>The mosque's north portal is marked by the density of its high-relief carving. Inside, stone piers support stone vaults, and the central bay was probably open to the sky. A wooden minbar of 1243 signed by Ibrahīm ibn Ahmad al-Tiflīsī survives. The hospital portal, on the west, is less dense and in places seems unfinished; rooms and iwans surround a covered courtyard with a pool, and one room served as a dynastic tomb chamber.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Inscriptions record several major restorations between the 15th and 19th centuries, and more followed in the 20th. The carving and eclectic architecture place the complex among the most important works of architecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and it was inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage List in 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Divriği Great Mosque and Hospital” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1228-1229 Ahmad Shah of Divrigi and his wife Turan Malik built the <b>Great Mosque and Hospital of Divrigi</b>, whose carved stone portals have few equals in medieval Islamic art.</blockquote><a href="/state/mengujekids">Mengujekids</a></li></ul></section>
